use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./CertJava import CertJava

export coding-rule CertJavaLck10J:
    standard: @{CertJava}
    ruleId: LCK10-J
    title: Use a correct form of the double-checked locking idiom
    statement:
        If you use double-checked locking for lazy initialization, declare
        the field volatile, or use a safer alternative such as the holder
        class idiom or static initialization.
    rationale:
        Without volatile, another thread may see a non-null reference to an
        object whose constructor has not finished, and act on a partly built
        object. CERT's risk assessment gives likelihood probable, detectable
        yes, repairable no, priority P4 and level L3.
    exceptions:
        The plain form is permitted, though discouraged, for 32-bit
        primitive values, because their reads and writes are atomic.
    severity: low
    noncompliant:
        ```java
        \\\
        private Helper helper;

        Helper getHelper() {
            if (helper == null) {
                synchronized (this) {
                    if (helper == null) helper = new Helper();
                }
            }
            return helper;
        }
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```java
        \\\
        private volatile Helper helper;

        Helper getHelper() {
            Helper h = helper;
            if (h == null) {
                synchronized (this) {
                    h = helper;
                    if (h == null) helper = h = new Helper();
                }
            }
            return h;
        }
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [java]
    tags: [concurrency, initialization, memory-model]
    source: https://cmu-sei.github.io/secure-coding-standards/sei-cert-oracle-coding-standard-for-java/rules/locking-lck/lck10-j/
